I'm curious about why Scala extracts class instance for one parameter(Foo1), but extracts parameters(Foo2(n,nn)) if their number > 1. Have I missed anything or it's intended behaviour?

Well I think, the main reason for introducing named based extractors is the possibility to use value classes (extending AnyVal) to extract a single value instead of def unapply(arg: Foo1): Options[Foo1]. This way you can avoid an instantiation of an extra Option and thus garbage collection. Usually, this is used to extract values that meet certain criteria.
Example:
class PositiveNonZeroInteger(private val n: Int) extends AnyVal {
  def isEmpty: Boolean = n <= 0
  def get: Int = n
}

object PositiveNonZeroInteger {
  def unapply(arg: Int) = new PositiveNonZeroInteger(arg)
}

1 match {
  case PositiveNonZeroInteger(pos) => "yay"
  case _                           => "nay"
} // => "yay"

0 match {
  case PositiveNonZeroInteger(pos) => "yay"
  case _                           => "nay"
} // => "nay"

It seems, the tuple getters (_1, _2) are probably only parsed by the compiler if at least two of them exist.
So if you'd rewrite your first foo to:
class Foo1(private val n: Int) /*extends AnyVal*/ {
  def isEmpty: Boolean = false
  def get: Int = n
}

object Foo1 {
  def unapply(arg: Foo1) = arg
}

Your code would work but this is not what name based extractors are for.
